course: biology
lesson: 84
title: The ape house: gorillas and chimpanzees
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In the ape house a huge silverback sits quietly, chewing leaves and watching his family, because real gorillas are mostly calm, shy plant-eaters, nothing like King Kong roaring on top of a skyscraper.
explain: When he beats his chest he uses cupped hands, not fists, and the pop carries through the forest to say «I am big, stay away», often stopping a fight before it starts.
explain: Next door the chimpanzees poke sticks into a fake termite mound to fish out honey and sit grooming each other, which is how chimps make friends and make up after a quarrel.
explain: Count the tails in the ape house and you will count none: apes have no tails, which is one way to tell them from monkeys.
fact: The silverback is the grown-up male leader of a gorilla group; the silver hair on his back appears at about 12 years old.
fact: Gorillas beat their chests with cupped hands, making a popping sound that can carry about a kilometre; it shows size and often prevents fights.
fact: Baby gorillas cling to their mothers and ride on their backs, and a mother has a baby only about every four years.
fact: Wild chimpanzees make tools: they fish for termites with stripped twigs, crack nuts with stones and use leaves as sponges.
fact: Chimps groom each other to remove dirt and bugs, but also to make friends and make up after fights.
fact: An adult chimpanzee is about a third stronger than a fit person of the same weight and can be dangerous, so chimps must never be pets.
fact: People and chimpanzees share about 98 to 99 per cent of their DNA in the parts that can be lined up; we are cousins sharing an ancestor from about 6 to 7 million years ago. All living things share the same basic cell machinery, so we even share some genes with a banana plant, which does not make us part banana.
fact: A long, hard stare can be a challenge in gorilla language, so keepers ask visitors not to stare at the gorillas.
fact: Real gorillas weigh up to about 200 kilograms and live mostly on the ground, seldom climbing high; a gorilla as big as a building could not exist, because its bones would break under its weight.
fact: Gorillas and other great apes build a new nest of leaves and branches every night, or of straw in a zoo.
fact: Zoo gorillas eat mostly leafy greens and vegetables; bananas are only treats, because sweet farm fruit has too much sugar for them and can harm their teeth and bellies.
fact: Apes, such as gorillas, chimpanzees and people, have no tails; most monkeys do.
exercise: What is a silverback? | The grown-up male leader of a gorilla group | A gorilla that has gone grey all over from very old age | A young gorilla with a white stripe | A kind of chimpanzee
exercise: What does a silverback do most of the day? | Eats, rests and keeps watch over his family | Hunts other animals in the forest for their meat | Fights the other gorillas | Climbs to the top of tall trees
exercise: How does a gorilla beat its chest? | With open, cupped hands | With closed fists | With a stick | With its feet
exercise: Why do gorillas beat their chests? | To show their size and often stop a fight before it starts | Because they are hungry and want the keeper to bring their dinner | To warm themselves up | To knock insects out of their fur
exercise: How do baby gorillas get around? | They cling to their mother and ride on her back | They are carried by the silverback in his big mouth | They walk on their own right from the very first day | They are pushed along the ground by older brothers
exercise: How often does a gorilla mother usually have a baby? | About every four years | Every year, in the spring | Twice every single year | Only once in her whole life
exercise: What does a chimp do with a stick at a fake termite mound? | It pokes it in to fish out honey or termites | It uses the stick to hit the other chimps when they come too close | It chews it like a toothbrush | It throws it at the visitors
exercise: Why do chimps groom each other? | To clean the fur and to make friends and make up | Only to find small things to eat in each other's fur | Because their own arms are too short | To change the colour of the fur
exercise: How strong is an adult chimpanzee compared with a person of the same weight? | About a third stronger | About ten times stronger | Weaker than a small child | Exactly the same as a person
exercise: How much of our DNA do we share with chimps in the parts that line up? | About 98 to 99 per cent | About 10 per cent | About 50 per cent | All of it
exercise: Did people come from chimpanzees? | No, we are cousins with a shared ancestor | Yes, chimps slowly turned into people | Yes, about a thousand years ago, in Africa | No, we are not related at all
exercise: Why does a keeper ask you not to stare at a gorilla? | A hard stare can be a challenge in gorilla language | Staring makes the gorilla fall asleep in the middle of the day | Gorillas cannot see people at all | Staring hurts the glass
exercise: Why could King Kong not exist? | Bones of an ape that size would break under its weight | Gorillas are afraid of tall buildings and would never climb one | Apes cannot climb at all | There is not enough food in cities
exercise: What does a gorilla do at bedtime? | Builds a nest of leaves or straw | Climbs into a hollow tree trunk | Sleeps standing up | Digs a deep burrow
exercise: What do zoo gorillas mostly eat? | Leafy greens and vegetables | Bananas and nothing else at all | Meat and fish | Bread and biscuits
exercise: How many tails does a gorilla have? | None | One short one | One long one | Two short ones
test: In King Kong a giant gorilla climbs a skyscraper and roars at planes. What would you fix? | Real gorillas are calm, about 200 kg, and seldom climb high | Right, gorillas often climb tall buildings when they get out of the zoo | Real gorillas are even bigger | Real gorillas eat aeroplanes
test: 3 gorillas and 2 chimps in the ape house. how many tails | 0 | 5 | 2 | 3
test: «gorilla does the chest thing with fists, like in the cartoon» What is right? | It uses cupped hands, which make a loud pop | Yes, always with closed fists, just like a boxer | It uses its feet | It hits a tree, not its chest
test: «chimps r cute, i want one as a pet» What is right? | Grown-up chimps are much stronger than people and can be dangerous | Fine, chimps stay small and gentle all their life, like a pet cat or dog | Fine, chimps are as safe as hamsters | Only if you feed them bananas
test: Your mate says «we come from chimps, they're our granddads». What is right? | Chimps are our cousins; we share an ancestor | Yes, chimps turned into people a long time ago | No, we are closer to dogs | Yes, but only Europeans did
test: «we share 60% genes with a banana so we're half banana lol» What is true? | All living things share basic cell machinery, so some genes match | Yes, people really are partly made of bananas, that's what it means | No, people share no genes with plants | It means we should eat only bananas
test: What would happen if a zoo fed its gorillas lots of sweet bananas every day? | Too much sugar could harm their teeth and bellies | They would grow much stronger and taller than wild gorillas | Nothing, bananas are their only real food | They would stop building nests
test: A cartoon monkey eats only bananas all day. Which is closer to the truth for a gorilla? | It eats mostly leaves, stems and shoots, with some fruit | It eats only bananas, like in cartoons | It eats mostly meat | It eats insects and nothing else
test: Count: 2 gorillas each make a new nest every night for a week. How many nests is that? | 14 | 7 | 2 | 9
test: Which of these would a chimp use as a tool? | A stone to crack nuts | A fork to eat its food with | A spade to dig a garden | A rope to tie up a boat
test: «why do the chimps keep picking at each other, got fleas or what» What is right? | Grooming cleans the fur and keeps friendships strong | Yes, all zoo chimps have fleas and are picking them off to eat | They are looking for bananas | They are fighting very slowly
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the chest-beating display of the gorilla. | To signal size and strength, frequently averting an actual fight | To dislodge parasites from the fur | To summon the keeper at the appointed hour of feeding each day | To regulate body temperature
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the relationship between humans and chimpanzees. | They are cousins descended from a common ancestor | Humans are the direct descendants of modern chimpanzees | Chimpanzees are descended from humans | The two share no part of their DNA whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of gorillas maintained in zoological collections? | It consists principally of leafy greens and vegetables | It consists exclusively of bananas | It consists principally of meat and fish | It consists of bread and confectionery supplied by visitors
exam: Determine the reason for which an animal of the dimensions of the fictional King Kong could not exist. | Its bones would fracture under the weight of its own body | It would be unable to find sufficiently tall buildings to climb | Its fur would be too heavy to grow | Its voice would be too loud for its own ears
